Chapter 10
Aizawa stood at the front of the classroom, capture weapon loose around his neck. “Basic Hero Training is a rescue exercise today. Off-campus facility. Get in your costumes and on the bus. We roll in ten.”
The class scrambled. Lockers slammed. Costumes went on in record time. They piled onto the transport, Aizawa taking the front seat while the rest filled the rows. The bus pulled away from U.A., the city thinning into highway.
Talk started before they’d cleared the gates.
Tsuyu turned in her seat, finger on her cheek. “Midoriya, your quirk is a lot like All Might’s, ribbit. Super strength, the way it wrecks your body when you use it.”
Kirishima leaned across the aisle, sharp teeth showing. “Nah, All Might doesn’t break himself. That’s the difference. Man, I wish mine was flashier. Hardening’s reliable, but it doesn’t exactly make the highlight reels.”
“It’s already strong enough to make you a top hero,” Midoriya said, notebook half-open on his lap. “You can tank hits that would take most people out. That’s huge in rescue work.”
Kirishima rubbed the back of his neck, still grinning. “Appreciate it, but Bakugo and Todoroki have the full package. Power, range, the kind of quirks that get you scouted on sight.”
Tsuyu blinked, calm. “Bakugo’s too angry to stay popular, ribbit. Heroes need people to trust them.”
Bakugo’s head snapped around, palms already crackling. “Say that again, frog-face! I’ll show you popular!”
Kaminari laughed from two seats back. “She’s got a point, man. Your personality’s doing more damage than your explosions sometimes.”
“SHUT UP, DUNCE FACE!”
Mina cackled. Jiro’s earjacks twitched with the volume. Iida’s engines idled as he tried to restore order. “Everyone, please keep the volume appropriate for a moving vehicle!”
Midoriya watched Bakugo get dog-piled by the class and felt the old script flip. In middle school no one had talked to Kacchan like this. Now the whole bus was doing it.
Mina twisted fully around, pink skin bright under the overhead lights. “Okay, enough about them. Curtis. You’ve been quiet this whole ride. What’s the real deal with your quirk? ‘Enhanced Physiology’ doesn’t cover punching zero-pointers into scrap.”
Heads turned. Uraraka’s cheeks warmed. Momo set her notes aside, curious. Todoroki’s mismatched eyes shifted over. Even Bakugo paused the glaring.
Curtis sat with one arm draped along the seat, expression easy. He shook his head. “I’m either quirkless or a superhuman. Before quirks existed, some people were just born past the normal line. Stronger. Faster. Tougher. Those lines mostly died out. Mine didn’t.” He shrugged one shoulder. “Or I’m not human at all and I’m actually a cryptid that wandered out of the mountains. Dealer’s choice.”
The bus went quiet for a second.
Mina snorted first. “Cryptid. Okay, I can work with that. Antlers on the costume make way more sense now.”
Kirishima barked a laugh. “Manly as hell either way! Mountain cryptid hero—people would eat that up!”
Jiro’s mouth twitched. “So the zero-pointer thing was just… you being you.”
“Pretty much.”
Uraraka smiled behind her hand, small. “That’s kind of amazing, though. No quirk factor at all?”
“None that anyone’s found.” Curtis’s tone stayed light. “Nezu has the file. It’s thin on purpose.”
Momo tilted her head. “If the ability is purely physical, then training methodology would differ from quirk-based enhancement. Have you noticed specific limits, or does it scale with conditioning?”
“Scales,” Curtis said. “Same as anyone. Just starts higher.”
Todoroki spoke, quiet. “And the cryptid part. Was that only a joke?”
Curtis’s mouth curved. “Mostly.”
Bakugo scoffed from the back. “Whatever. Extra’s still an extra. Cryptid or not, I’m blowing past you at the Sports Festival.”
“Looking forward to it,” Curtis answered, easy.
Aizawa’s eye flicked up in the rearview, unreadable, then back to the road. The bus kept rolling. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet while the class turned his answer over, the highway stretching ahead toward the USJ and whatever rescue scenarios waited inside it.
Curtis smiled, small and easy, as the bus hummed along.
“Speaking of the Festival. Have any of you—besides Bakugo—actually tasted a real loss yet?” He glanced around the seats. “Sooner or later you’re going to hit that wall. Disappointment. The kind that sits in your chest after you gave everything and it still wasn’t enough.” His accent stayed low. “Remember this, though. A loss only pushes you further if you let it. Bakugo’s probably going to make it deep. Maybe even take the whole thing. He’s got the drive for it.”
The bus quieted again. Bakugo’s eyes narrowed, but he didn’t fire back immediately. Midoriya’s pencil had stopped. Kirishima nodded slowly, thoughtful. Uraraka’s hands rested in her lap, the words landing softer than she expected.
Mina broke the silence with a grin. “Wow. Cryptid wisdom on the way to rescue training. Love that for us.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh and leaned back. “Just saying. Don’t fold the first time it hurts.”
Aizawa’s eye flicked up in the mirror once more, then returned to the road. The USJ exit was coming up. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed exactly where it always was.
The bus rolled to a stop outside a massive domed facility. The class filed out into the open air, costumes bright under the midday sun. Waiting at the entrance stood Thirteen in her full space suit, helmet tilted in a friendly wave.
“Welcome to the Unforeseen Simulation Joint!” Her voice carried, warm and clear through the modulator. “I’m Thirteen. This facility is designed to train you for real rescue scenarios—landslides, shipwrecks, fires, the works. Today you’ll learn how to use your quirks to save lives, not just fight villains. Remember: a hero’s power can hurt as easily as it helps. Control matters more than raw strength when civilians are involved.”
The class murmured agreement. Midoriya was already taking mental notes. Uraraka nodded, focused.
Curtis stood near the back of the group, arms loose. Thirteen’s words sat wrong in his head.
If you’ve got the strength to end the threat, you end it. Saving people is the point, but leaving the thing that hurt them still standing just means more people get hurt later. Mountains didn’t care about restraint.
He spoke up, voice even. “With respect. If someone has the power to stop the danger completely, they should use it. Rescue’s important. So is making sure the problem doesn’t get back up.”
Thirteen’s helmet tilted. The class turned. Mina’s eyebrows rose. Todoroki’s eyes narrowed slightly. Aizawa, still by the bus, watched without interrupting.
Thirteen’s tone stayed kind. “That’s a valid perspective in some situations, Beardy. But many quirks aren’t precise enough for that without collateral. Today’s lesson is about the moments when saving has to come first. You’ll see why inside.”
Curtis nodded once. He didn’t push. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while Thirteen gestured them toward the doors and the simulated disasters waiting under the dome.
The lights inside the USJ flickered. A black-purple warp gate tore open in the central plaza. Villains poured through—dozens of them, low-level and armed, spreading across the simulated zones. Three figures stepped out last.
Tomura Shigaraki scratched at his neck, red eyes scanning the student group. “All Might’s not here.” His voice was dry, disappointed. “Schedule said he was teaching today. Hand it over, Kurogiri.”
Kurogiri’s mist form shifted. “The itinerary was clear. He should be present.”
Aizawa’s eyes narrowed. “The same ones who broke the barrier yesterday.” He didn’t hesitate. “Thirteen—evacuate the students. Contact the school. Now.”
Curtis was already moving, mask clicking into place from the compact form in his belt. Antlers locked. Eye slits lit—one red, one blue. Crypt King. He fell in beside Aizawa without a word.
Midoriya’s voice jumped. “Mr. Aizawa—Beardy—you can’t take all of them alone—!”
“We can,” Aizawa said, capture weapon already unwinding. “Go.”
They leapt. A firing line of villains raised quirks—fire, ice, kinetic blasts. Aizawa’s eyes flared red. Erasure killed every emitter at once. His scarf snapped out in precise arcs, binding three before they could recover. Curtis hit the rest. Two steps, three clean strikes. Jaws, temples, solar plexus. Bodies dropped without theatrics.
“Eraser Head,” one villain snarled, recognizing the goggles and scarf. “Quirk’s erasure—won’t work on me!”
A mutant with reinforced arms charged. Aizawa kept him at range, scarf looping limbs, never letting the distance close. Another mutant broke for Curtis, fists swinging. Curtis met the first punch, slipped the second, and drove two short shots into the side of the head. The villain’s neck snapped one way, then the other. He collapsed.
On the stairs, Midoriya stared. “I… underestimated them.”
“Move!” Iida’s engines flared. “Thirteen is waiting!”
The class turned to run. Kurogiri’s warp opened across the path, mist spreading. “We are the League of Villains. Our purpose is All Might’s death. You children are simply the means.”
Bakugo and Kirishima launched forward—explosion and hardened fist. Both passed through mist and reappeared nowhere useful. Thirteen raised her glove. Before Black Hole could fully open, Kurogiri warped again.
The class vanished in pieces—scattered into shipwreck, landslide, fire, flood. Curtis felt the mist close around him mid-stride. One second he was beside Aizawa. The next the plaza was gone.
He hit unfamiliar ground alone, mask still on, the low steady pull in his chest unchanged, while somewhere above the dome the rest of the fight continued without him.
The warp closed. Curtis was gone. The plaza held only Aizawa, the remaining villains, and the three at the center.
Shigaraki’s fingers paused on his neck. He stared at the empty space where the tall, antlered figure had been, then at Aizawa. The height. The build. The way the masked one had moved like he belonged in the fight instead of running from it. Not a student. Faculty.
“Now, Eraser Head.” Shigaraki’s voice stayed dry, almost bored. “Let’s see how you fare without your sidekick and the kids.”
Aizawa’s capture weapon shifted, eyes still red. He didn’t correct the assumption. “You brought this many for one hero. Overkill usually means you’re compensating.”
Shigaraki’s mouth twitched under the hand. “All Might was supposed to be here. You’ll do for a warm-up.” He glanced at Kurogiri. “Keep the exits sealed. I want to watch him work without the extra help.”
The remaining villains closed in. Aizawa set his feet, scarf ready, and didn’t look toward the zones where his students—and the one Shigaraki had mistaken for faculty—had been scattered. The low light of the dome hummed. The real fight in the plaza was just starting.
The warp spat Curtis out onto cracked concrete at the edge of the landslide zone. Dust hung in the air. When it cleared, he wasn’t alone.
A loose ring of villains had already closed around the landing point—fifteen, maybe twenty. Mix of emitter and mutant types. Weapons drawn. Quirks humming. They’d seen the antlers and the mask and decided the tall one in the bone skull was worth swarming before he could find his footing.
Crypt King straightened. The deer-skull mask sat level, eye slits glowing steady red and blue. Braids hung against the dark scaled armor. The tattered cloak shifted once in the stale facility air. He rolled his neck. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet.
One of the villains—a woman with blade-like fingers—grinned. “Faculty straggler. Or a cosplayer. Either way, you’re surrounded.”
Curtis didn’t answer. He set his feet, armored boots grinding concrete, and raised his hands. The circle tightened. Someone laughed. Someone else charged.
He met the first one halfway.
The woman with the blade fingers circled closer, metal edges catching the facility lights. Her grin stayed sharp.
“No one will hear you scream. No one will see you die here.”
Crypt King’s mask tilted. The red and blue slits stayed level on her. “Really?”
She paused, blades flexing. “Hmm?”
“No one can see or hear me right now?” His voice came even through the filter, almost curious. The armored shoulders rolled once. “That’s what you’re saying.”
The ring of villains shifted. A few exchanged looks. The woman’s grin faltered at the edges, like the answer hadn’t landed the way she expected. Crypt King didn’t move yet. He just stood in the center of them, cloak still, waiting to see if she understood what she’d just handed him.
They all dashed at once. “Exactly!”
Curtis shut his red eye. Only the blue slit stayed lit, cold and steady through the mask. The charging villains closed the last meters—and none of them touched him.
Footsteps slid past his shoulders. Blades cut air where his ribs had been. A mutant’s fist phased through the space his head occupied a half-second earlier. He stood exactly where he’d started, cloak barely stirring, while every strike arrived late.
Then the music hit.
It started in their skulls—no speakers, no source. A low, driving electronic pulse. Familiar to anyone who’d ever heard the track. Megalovania. The notes locked into their heads and wouldn’t leave. Timing warped. Depth perception slipped. Every lunge felt a beat behind.
Crypt King’s voice cut through the song, calm. “You’re all in for a really bad time. I’m going to tell you a secret you’re all going to die with. I have multiple quirks. This one is called Megalovania.”
Bones erupted around him. White, sharp, orbiting in clean rings—some long, some short, all moving with the music’s rhythm. They didn’t attack yet. They just circled, a spinning barrier that forced the villains to weave or get clipped.
The group kept coming. Punches, quirks, blades. Nothing landed. Curtis shifted only when he felt like it—one step, a tilt of the antlers—and the strikes passed through empty space again. The bones spun faster. The music climbed.
“Now then,” he said, blue eye bright. “Let’s play.”
The first bone shot forward. The villains were still trying to hit something that refused to be where their hands expected it.
The bones answered the music.
They shot outward in time with the beat—short volleys that forced the villains to break formation. One blade-fingered woman ducked a rib and still took a grazing cut across her arm. A mutant with extra limbs tried to bull through the ring and caught three bones through the shoulder before he staggered back. The song in their heads kept climbing. Footing slipped. Depth lied.
Crypt King moved inside the orbit. Not fast in the Blink way. Just absent from the places they swung. A kick meant for his knee passed through cloak. A fire blast scorched the concrete where he’d stood a second earlier. He answered with a closed fist that dropped the pyro in one clean shot to the temple, then stepped away before the body hit.
“Stay with the rhythm,” he said, voice even under the mask. Another bone lanced through a charging villain’s calf and pinned him. “Or don’t. Doesn’t change the ending.”
They tried to swarm. Fifteen left, then twelve. The blades woman screamed and lunged with both hands extended. Curtis tilted his head. The antlers cleared her strike by inches. His palm met her chest and drove her back into two of her own allies. Bones followed, pinning all three to the cracked floor.
The music hit its hardest stretch. The remaining villains’ movements turned sloppy—late, overcommitted, panicked. Crypt King walked through them. One precise strike per body. Jaw. Solar plexus. Base of the skull. No wasted motion. The bones harassed anyone who tried to regroup, keeping the circle broken.
When the last one dropped, the song cut.
Silence rushed back into the landslide zone. Bones dissolved into white dust that faded before it touched the ground. Curtis stood in the center of the unconscious pile, blue eye still lit, red one still shut. He opened it again. Both slits glowed. He rolled his neck once.
“Bad time,” he said to no one.
The low, steady pull in his chest stayed exactly where it always was. Somewhere else in the dome, the rest of the class was still fighting. He turned toward the nearest exit path and started walking.
Curtis raised both hands.
The unconscious villains jerked. Concrete split under them. They were dragged downward in a single grinding pull—bodies, gear, and all—until the ground sealed shut over the last of them. No blood on the surface. Just fresh cracks and silence.
Dead. Their bones stayed behind, folded into the earth like rebar. Megalovania could use what the dead left. He could have called those bones back up as weapons. Could have run beams through them—concentrated, cutting, worse than the orbiting volleys. He hadn’t needed to. The fight ended before the quirk’s full shape showed.
The cost sat in his stance. Megalovania drained everything defensive and poured it into the rest—speed, timing, the untouchable gaps, the bone control. One clean hit from any of them and he would have felt it all the way through. No armor rating. No braced tolerance. Just the music and the math. He’d kept the gap perfect so it never mattered.
The blue eye dimmed to match the red. Both slits glowed steady again. Curtis lowered his hands. The landslide zone was empty except for him and the new seams in the concrete. He rolled one shoulder, cloak settling, and turned toward the path that led back to the central plaza.
The low, steady pull in his chest remained exactly where it always was. Somewhere ahead, Aizawa was still fighting. Curtis started walking.
A thin layer of warped air shattered around him as he cleared the landslide edge. One of the villains had tried a spatial snare on the way out. It hadn’t held.
Curtis took in the dome from the high path. Students fought in scattered pockets across the zones—flashes of quirks, shouts, the occasional explosion. He cracked his neck, mask steady.
“You’re all doing fantastic. Plus Ultra!”
He jumped. The drop carried him toward the Shipwreck Zone, boots hitting a half-submerged walkway at a run.
Below, the ruined ship listed. Villains had it surrounded, water quirks churning the artificial lake, but they hadn’t boarded. Izuku stood on the tilting deck with Tsuyu and Minoru, voice low and fast as he laid out the plan. Minoru’s grape spheres popped in nervous clusters. Tsuyu’s eyes tracked the waterline.
A villain raised both arms. A pressurized wave slammed the hull and split it. The ship began to sink.
Izuku moved. He leapt, planted, and drove a Delaware Smash into the surface. The water exploded outward, then rushed back in a violent convergence. Minoru’s spheres went with it, tangling the villains into a single sticky, thrashing mass. Tsuyu’s tongue snapped out, wrapped both boys, and yanked them clear as the wreck slid under.
Two more villains surfaced behind them, water still streaming off mutated limbs, already lunging for the escaping trio.
Curtis hit the lake like a dropped anchor. He crashed between the pair, armored hands locking onto collars and shoulders, and dragged them straight under. The surface closed over all three. Bubbles and thrashing limbs broke for a second, then went still.
When he came back up, the two villains were limp in his grip. He hauled them onto a floating section of hull and left them there. The mask turned toward the trio on the far platform.
“You good?”
Tsuyu blinked, tongue still half-extended. Minoru stared. Izuku’s eyes were wide behind the water spray.
“Y-yeah,” Izuku managed. “We’re good.”
Curtis nodded once and turned toward the next zone, water streaming off the cloak. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the Shipwreck Zone settled behind him.
Curtis paused on the floating hull, water still dripping from the cloak. He looked at Midoriya—green hair plastered, costume scorched at the gloves, breathing hard but steady.
“You’re so incredibly strong, Midoriya. I’d love to spar with you sometime. I could learn a lot from you.”
Midoriya’s eyes went wide. Color climbed his neck despite the cold water. “E-eh?! Me? I—I mean, you’re the one who just—” He gestured helplessly at the two unconscious villains. “I’d be honored! Really! Whenever you want!”
Tsuyu’s head tilted, a small smile on her face. “Ribbit. That’s high praise from someone who cleared a whole zone alone.”
Minoru just stared, grape spheres forgotten. “He wants to spar you? After that?”
Curtis’s mouth curved under the mask. “You read the fight and ended it with what you had. That’s the part worth learning.” He straightened, already turning toward the central plaza path. “Stay with the group. I’ll clear what’s left between here and the exit.”
He stepped off the hull and onto the next walkway, leaving Midoriya flushed and nodding hard while Tsuyu gently pulled both boys toward safer ground. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed exactly where it always was.
